From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 00:21:20 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 23/24] script: add SVGStyleElement, so <style> inside SVG
 registers

A <style> element inside an <svg> fell back to a bare SVGElement, so its
text was never parsed and the sheet never registered:
document.styleSheets did not grow, and every rule inside it silently did
nothing. That is how Mermaid ships its entire theme -- a <style> block
inside the generated SVG -- which is why a themed diagram painted as
solid black shapes with invisible strokes even once everything else
worked.

This is the trimmed part of HTMLStyleElement a style-in-SVG needs: parse
the text content, register the sheet, keep it current across children
and type-attribute mutations, remove it on unbind. No CSSOM sheet
accessor, no media reflection, no render-blocking -- none of which
change whether the rules cascade. The sheet is parsed directly rather
than through StylesheetContentsCache, whose loader plumbing is typed to
HTMLElement; the cost is re-parsing identical sheets, and @import is not
loaded.

The inline CSP check is deliberately kept even though everything else
optional was dropped: a <style> arriving inside SVG markup is exactly
the kind of content a policy means to constrain, and the app embedding
this engine documents that concern about Mermaid specifically.

The type attribute is honored -- empty or text/css only -- because the
first version ignored it and immediately regressed two subtests of
styling/attr-style-type-dynamic.html that assert a type="text/plain"
sheet must not apply. That test now passes 3/4 against 2/7 at baseline,
the remainder being type-attribute IDL reflection this element does not
implement.

With this plus foreignObject layout, Mermaid renders: themed nodes sized
to their labels, readable text, edges. Arrowheads are still missing
because <marker> is not implemented.
